## 3. Splunk Installation & Setup

### System Requirements
- **OS**: Linux (RHEL, CentOS, Ubuntu), Windows, macOS
- **RAM**: Minimum 4 GB (recommended 12+ GB for production)
- **Disk**: Depends on data volume; SSDs recommended for hot/warm buckets
- **CPU**: Multi-core recommended

### Linux Installation
```bash
# Download the RPM or tar package from splunk.com
# RPM installation
sudo rpm -i splunk-<version>.rpm

# Start Splunk
sudo /opt/splunk/bin/splunk start --accept-license

# Enable boot start
sudo /opt/splunk/bin/splunk enable boot-start
```

### Windows Installation
- Download the MSI installer from splunk.com
- Run the installer and follow the wizard
- Splunk installs to `C:\Program Files\Splunk` by default
- Access via `http://localhost:8000`

### Universal Forwarder Installation
```bash
# Linux
sudo rpm -i splunkforwarder-<version>.rpm
sudo /opt/splunkforwarder/bin/splunk start --accept-license

# Configure forwarding
/opt/splunkforwarder/bin/splunk add forward-server <indexer_ip>:9997
/opt/splunkforwarder/bin/splunk add monitor /var/log/
```

### Key Configuration Files
| File | Location | Purpose |
|------|----------|---------|
| `inputs.conf` | `$SPLUNK_HOME/etc/system/local/` | Define data inputs (files, ports, scripts) |
| `outputs.conf` | `$SPLUNK_HOME/etc/system/local/` | Define where to forward data |
| `props.conf` | `$SPLUNK_HOME/etc/system/local/` | Define source types, parsing rules, timestamps |
| `transforms.conf` | `$SPLUNK_HOME/etc/system/local/` | Define field extractions, lookups, routing rules |
| `indexes.conf` | `$SPLUNK_HOME/etc/system/local/` | Define indexes and their storage policies |
| `server.conf` | `$SPLUNK_HOME/etc/system/local/` | General server settings |
| `web.conf` | `$SPLUNK_HOME/etc/system/local/` | Web UI settings |
| `authorize.conf` | `$SPLUNK_HOME/etc/system/local/` | Role-based access control |
| `authentication.conf` | `$SPLUNK_HOME/etc/system/local/` | Authentication settings (LDAP, SAML) |

### Configuration File Precedence (highest to lowest)
1. `$SPLUNK_HOME/etc/system/local/` (user overrides — highest priority)
2. `$SPLUNK_HOME/etc/apps/<app_name>/local/`
3. `$SPLUNK_HOME/etc/apps/<app_name>/default/`
4. `$SPLUNK_HOME/etc/system/default/` (factory defaults — lowest priority)

